<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shan State</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shan State is the largest state of Myanmar, covering the Shan Plateau in the east of the country. It was formed in 1948 from the Federated Shan States when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> became independent, after Shan leaders agreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Conference</a> of 1947 to join the new Union. In 1959 the hereditary saophas gave up their ruling powers to the Shan State government, ending the old principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shan-state.jpg" alt="Shan State" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Fussan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pindaya_Caves_0001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tai Tham script spread from Chiang Mai to the Tai Lue of Sipsong Panna in Yunnan, the Tai Khun of Kengtung in the <b>Shan State</b> and Lan Xang, where it became the standard script for Buddhist texts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1950s Chinese Nationalist (Kuomintang) troops who had fled into <b>Shan State</b> added another war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Panglong Conference of 1947 Shan leaders agreed with Aung San to join an independent Burma, and the Federated <b>Shan States</b> became Shan State of the Union of Burma in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a></li><li><blockquote>Kengtung remains the main town of eastern <b>Shan State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kengtung-state">Kengtung State</a></li><li><blockquote>Like other saophas, its ruler surrendered his powers to the <b>Shan State</b> government in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/momeik-state">Momeik State</a></li><li><blockquote>North Hsenwi became part of <b>Shan State</b> in independent Burma in 1948, and its saopha surrendered his powers in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-hsenwi">North Hsenwi</a></li></ul></section>
